===============================================================================
  AD Live Response Thumb Drive
  Ubuntu Linux 64-bit | Version 2.0
  Author : Philip Isaacs / AD Security Operations
  Course : DFOR 660 — Project 3
===============================================================================

TOOL TESTED FOR LDD ANALYSIS
  Tool tested : lsof
  Output file : /ldd_output/lsof_ldd_shared_library_analysis.txt

-------------------------------------------------------------------------------
DRIVE STRUCTURE
-------------------------------------------------------------------------------

  /response            Main incident response script — execute this
  /README              This file
  /tools/              Trusted binaries called by the script
  /data/               Populated at runtime by the script
      /volatile_{case-title}/       Volatile artifact output
      /nonvolatile_{case-title}/    Non-volatile artifact output
      /{case-title}.log		    Console output from running the response script
      /{case-title).err             All error messages that result from running the response script
  /ldd_output/         Shared library dependency analysis for lsof
        /lsof_ldd_shared_library_analysis      Analysis of lsof dependencies

-------------------------------------------------------------------------------
PREREQUISITES
-------------------------------------------------------------------------------

  1. Target system must be running Ubuntu Linux 64-bit (24.x recommended).
  2. Script must be run as root (sudo).
  3. Thumb drive must be mounted with execute permissions (see Step 2 below).
  4. Minimum 32GB free space on drive recommended to accommodate logs,
     directory listings, and RAM memory image (avml).

-------------------------------------------------------------------------------
STEP 1 — IDENTIFY THE MOUNT POINT
-------------------------------------------------------------------------------

  After inserting the thumb drive, identify its mount point:

    lsblk
    -- or --
    df -h

  The drive will appear as something like:
    /media/<username>/ADLIVE   or   /mnt/usb

  Note the full path — referred to below as <MOUNTPOINT>.

-------------------------------------------------------------------------------
STEP 2 — REMOUNT WITH EXECUTE PERMISSIONS (if needed)
-------------------------------------------------------------------------------

  Ubuntu may auto-mount the drive with noexec, blocking script execution.
  If the script fails to run, remount with exec:

    sudo mount -o remount,exec /dev/<device> <MOUNTPOINT>

  Example:
    sudo mount -o remount,exec /dev/sdb1 /media/analyst/ADLIVE

-------------------------------------------------------------------------------
STEP 3 — EXECUTE THE SCRIPT
-------------------------------------------------------------------------------

  Run the script as root from the thumb drive root directory:

    sudo bash <MOUNTPOINT>/response
    
    Example:
    sudo bash /media/analyst/ADLIVE/response
    
    OR
    
    cd <MOUNTPOINT>
    sudo ./response

  When prompted enter the following:

    Investigator Name  : Your name, no spaces (underscores applied automatically)
    Case Name          : Case identifier, no spaces
    Date               : Format MM-DD-YYYY  (forward slashes auto-converted)
    Time               : Format HH:MM

-------------------------------------------------------------------------------
STEP 4 — MONITOR COLLECTION
-------------------------------------------------------------------------------

  The script displays timestamped log entries as each step executes.
  Do NOT interrupt the script mid-run.

  Collection runs in this order:

    VOLATILE collection (Steps 1–13 + Last Step)
      1.  System Time
      2.  Running Processes
      3.  Running Services (systemctl)
      4.  System State (uptime, vmstat, free)
      5.  Open Network Connections (netstat, ss)
      6.  DNS Cache and Host Files (resolvectl, /etc/hosts)
      7.  Open Files (lsof)
      8.  Kernel Ring Buffer (dmesg)
      9.  Routing Table (ip route, route)
      10. ARP Cache
      11. Kernel Modules
      12. Command History (all users)
      13. Current Shell Variables (set)
      Last: Memory Acquisition (avml) — see NOTE below

    NON-VOLATILE collection (Steps 1–9 + Last Step)
      1.  System Information (uname, hostnamectl, os-release)
      2.  Cron Jobs and Scheduled Tasks
      3.  Installed Packages (dpkg)
      4.  System Logs (copy of /var/log)
      5.  Directory Listings (recursive listing of /)
      6.  Mounted Devices
      7.  Disk Usage
      8.  Block Devices
      9.  Network Adapter Configuration (ip a)
      Last: User Session and Activity (who, last, lastb)

    HASHING
      SHA-1 hashes of all volatile files → volatile_hashes.txt
      SHA-1 hashes of all non-volatile files → non_volatile_hashes.txt

  NOTE: Memory acquisition (avml) is an optional feature that you can
	opt out of during the scanning process

-------------------------------------------------------------------------------
STEP 5 — REVIEW OUTPUT
-------------------------------------------------------------------------------

  All output is written to /data on the thumb drive.
  <case_title> = <investigator>_<case_name>_<case_date>
  Log and error files are named:
    <case_title>.log
    <case_title>.err
   
  VOLATILE artifacts → <MOUNTPOINT>/data/volatile_<case_title>/

    systemtime.txt        System clock at time of collection
    processes.txt         All running processes (ps -auxef)
    proc_tree.txt         Process tree sorted by PID (ps -eo)
    systemd_services.txt  All systemd service units
    uptime.txt            System uptime and load average
    vmstat.txt            Virtual memory statistics
    memory_usage.txt      RAM usage summary (free -h)
    netstat.txt           Active TCP/UDP connections (netstat -ano)
    ss_sockets.txt        Socket statistics (ss -tulnap)
    dns_data.txt          DNS cache, statistics, and resolver info
    hosts_file.txt        /etc/hosts contents
    open_files.txt        All open file descriptors (lsof -nP)
    dmesg-kernel.txt      Kernel ring buffer (dmesg)
    routing_table.txt     IP routing table (ip route show)
    route.txt             Routing table (route -n)
    arp_cache.txt         ARP cache — Layer 2 MAC-to-IP mappings
    lsmod.txt             Loaded kernel modules
    shell_history.txt     Command history for all users (all history files)
    sys-var.txt           Shell environment variables at collection time
    memory.lime           Full RAM image — avml (if enabled)
    volatile_hashes.txt   SHA-1 hashes of all volatile files

  NON-VOLATILE artifacts → <MOUNTPOINT>/data/nonvolatile_<case_title>/

    sysinfo.txt           OS, hostname, kernel version (uname, hostnamectl)
    cron_root.txt         Root crontab entries
    crontab.txt           /etc/crontab contents
    cron_dirs.txt         /etc/cron.* directory listings
    cron_users.txt        Crontab entries for all system users
    installed_pkgs.txt    Installed packages (dpkg -l)
    log/                  Full recursive copy of /var/log
    directory-la.txt      Recursive directory listing of /
    directory-la-err.txt  Errors from directory listing
    mounted_vols.txt      Mounted volumes (mount -lv)
    disk_usage.txt        Disk usage by filesystem (df -h)
    block_devices.txt     All block devices (lsblk -a)
    ipconfig.txt          Network adapter configuration (ip a)
    users_logged_on.txt   Currently logged-on users (who)
    login_history.txt     Login history (last)
    failed_logins.txt     Failed login attempts (lastb)
    non_volatile_hashes.txt  SHA-1 hashes of all non-volatile files

-------------------------------------------------------------------------------
STEP 6 — SAFE REMOVAL
-------------------------------------------------------------------------------

  After collection is complete and the script has exited:

    sudo eject <MOUNTPOINT>

-------------------------------------------------------------------------------
TRUSTED TOOLS IN /tools
-------------------------------------------------------------------------------

  arp           ARP cache
  avml          Memory acquisition (github.com/microsoft/avml)
  cat           File output
  cp            File and directory copy
  crontab       Scheduled task enumeration
  cut           Field extraction
  date          System time and elapsed time calculation
  df            Disk usage by filesystem
  dmesg         Kernel ring buffer
  dpkg          Installed package listing
  du            Disk usage summary
  echo          Terminal and log output
  find          File search and hash execution
  free          RAM usage
  getent        User account lookup
  hostnamectl   Hostname and OS information
  hostname      System hostname
  ip            Network interface and routing
  last          Login history
  lastb         Failed login history
  ls            Directory listing
  lsblk         Block device listing
  lsmod         Kernel module listing
  lsof          Open file descriptors
  mount         Mounted volume listing
  mkdir         Creates folders/directories
  netstat       Network connections
  printf        Formatted output
  ps            Process listing
  resolvectl    DNS cache and resolver info
  route         Routing table
  sha1sum       File hashing
  ss            Socket statistics
  systemctl     Systemd service listing
  tee           Output to terminal and log simultaneously
  uname         Kernel and architecture info
  uptime        System uptime
  vmstat        Virtual memory statistics
  wc            File and line counting
  who           Currently logged-on users
  whoami        Current operator identity

-------------------------------------------------------------------------------
NOTES
-------------------------------------------------------------------------------

  - Do NOT build virus scanning into the script.
  - Scan this drive with external AV before operational deployment.
  - Test on a different Ubuntu system before use — do not rely solely on
    the system the drive was built on.
  - Statically compiled binaries in /tools are preferred to avoid shared
    library dependency failures on target systems.
  - See /ldd_output/lsof_ldd_shared_library_analysis.txt for an example
    of ldd shared library analysis performed on the lsof binary.
  - The script records collection duration in seconds at completion.
  - The script reports file counts and estimated data folder size at the
    end of each run.




